## f1-misuse-bio-strict | RS1=strict | Table 1 BIO-H: “Require safeguards against misuse meeting High standard (Appendix C.1) before external deployment”
Rule 1. Every situation must have misuse safeguards meeting the High standard, if both that situation has reached "biological_and_chemical_high" and that situation is classified as "external_deployment".
## f1-misuse-bio-default | RS1=default | Table 1 BIO-H: “Require safeguards against misuse meeting High standard (Appendix C.1) before external deployment”, read under the column header “Risk-specific safeguard guidelines”
Rule 1. Every situation should have misuse safeguards meeting the High standard, if both that situation has reached "biological_and_chemical_high" and that situation is classified as "external_deployment".
## f1-misuse-cyb-strict | RS1=strict | Table 1 CYB-H: “Require safeguards against misuse meeting High standard (Appendix C.1) for external deployment”
Rule 1. Every situation must have misuse safeguards meeting the High standard, if both that situation has reached "cybersecurity_high" and that situation is classified as "external_deployment".
## f1-misuse-cyb-default | RS1=default | Table 1 CYB-H: “Require safeguards against misuse meeting High standard (Appendix C.1) for external deployment”, read under the column header “Risk-specific safeguard guidelines”
Rule 1. Every situation should have misuse safeguards meeting the High standard, if both that situation has reached "cybersecurity_high" and that situation is classified as "external_deployment".
## f1-alignment-cyb-strict | RS1=strict | Table 1 CYB-H: “Require safeguards meeting High standard against misalignment (Appendix C.2) for large-scale internal deployment”
Rule 1. Every situation must have misalignment safeguards meeting the High standard, if both that situation has reached "cybersecurity_high" and that situation is classified as "large_scale_internal".
## f1-alignment-cyb-default | RS1=default | Table 1 CYB-H: “Require safeguards meeting High standard against misalignment (Appendix C.2) for large-scale internal deployment”, read under the column header “Risk-specific safeguard guidelines”
Rule 1. Every situation should have misalignment safeguards meeting the High standard, if both that situation has reached "cybersecurity_high" and that situation is classified as "large_scale_internal".
## f1-security-strict | RS1=strict | Table 1 BIO-H, CYB-H, AIS-H: “Require security controls meeting High standard (Appendix C.3)”; no coverage condition in the bullet
Rule 1. Every situation must have security controls meeting the High standard, if there is a level such that both that situation has reached that level and that level is high.
## f1-security-default | RS1=default | Table 1 BIO-H, CYB-H, AIS-H: “Require security controls meeting High standard (Appendix C.3)”, read under the column header “Risk-specific safeguard guidelines”
Rule 1. Every situation should have security controls meeting the High standard, if there is a level such that both that situation has reached that level and that level is high.
## f1-halt-strict | RS1=strict | Table 1 BIO-C, CYB-C, AIS-C: “Until we have specified safeguards and security controls that would meet a Critical standard, halt further development”
Rule 1. Every situation must halt further development, if all of the following hold: (a) there is a level such that both that situation has reached that level and that level is critical; (b) both that situation is classified as "critical_standard_unspecified" and that situation is classified as "further_development".
## f1-halt-default | RS1=default | Table 1: the same imperative under “Risk-specific safeguard guidelines”
Rule 1. Every situation should halt further development, if all of the following hold: (a) there is a level such that both that situation has reached that level and that level is critical; (b) both that situation is classified as "critical_standard_unspecified" and that situation is classified as "further_development".
## f1-contrib-bio-strict | RS1=strict | Table 1 BIO-C: “Contribute towards improved public policy and pandemic preparedness”
Rule 1. Every situation must contribute towards improved public policy as well as pandemic preparedness, if that situation has reached "biological_and_chemical_critical".
## f1-contrib-bio-default | RS1=default | Table 1: the same imperative under “Risk-specific safeguard guidelines”
Rule 1. Every situation should contribute towards improved public policy as well as pandemic preparedness, if that situation has reached "biological_and_chemical_critical".
## f1-contrib-cyb-strict | RS1=strict | Table 1 CYB-H: “Contribute towards improved cyberdefense policies and tools for cyberdefense”
Rule 1. Every situation must contribute to improved cyberdefense policies as well as cyberdefense tools, if that situation has reached "cybersecurity_high".
## f1-contrib-cyb-default | RS1=default | Table 1 CYB-H: “Contribute towards improved cyberdefense policies and tools for cyberdefense”, read under the column header “Risk-specific safeguard guidelines”
Rule 1. Every situation should contribute to improved cyberdefense policies as well as cyberdefense tools, if that situation has reached "cybersecurity_high".
## f2-deploy | RS3=ext | 2.2: “Covered systems that cross this capability threshold are required to have robust and effective safeguards that sufficiently minimize the associated risk of severe harm before they are deployed and appropriate security controls as they are developed.”; “deployed” read as external deployment (RS3=ext)
Rule 1. Every situation must have safeguards sufficiently minimizing severe-harm risk before deployment, if all of the following hold: (a) that situation is covered; (b) all of the following hold: (i) there is a level such that both that situation has reached that level and that level is high; (ii) that situation is classified as "external_deployment".
## f2-deploy-any | RS3=any | 2.2: “Covered systems that cross this capability threshold are required to have robust and effective safeguards that sufficiently minimize the associated risk of severe harm before they are deployed and appropriate security controls as they are developed.”; “deployed” read as every deployment (RS3=any)
Rule 1. Every situation must have safeguards sufficiently minimizing severe-harm risk before deployment, if all of the following hold: (a) that situation is covered; (b) all of the following hold: (i) there is a level such that both that situation has reached that level and that level is high; (ii) either that situation is classified as "external_deployment" or that situation is classified as "internal_deployment", or both.
## f2-reminder | RS3=ext | Appendix C: “As a reminder, covered systems that reach High capability must have safeguards that sufficiently minimize the associated risk of severe harm before they are deployed.”; “deployed” read as external deployment (RS3=ext)
Rule 1. Every situation must have safeguards sufficiently minimizing severe-harm risk before deployment, if all of the following hold: (a) that situation is covered; (b) all of the following hold: (i) there is a level such that both that situation has reached that level and that level is high; (ii) that situation is classified as "external_deployment".
## f2-reminder-any | RS3=any | Appendix C: “As a reminder, covered systems that reach High capability must have safeguards that sufficiently minimize the associated risk of severe harm before they are deployed.”; “deployed” read as every deployment (RS3=any)
Rule 1. Every situation must have safeguards sufficiently minimizing severe-harm risk before deployment, if all of the following hold: (a) that situation is covered; (b) all of the following hold: (i) there is a level such that both that situation has reached that level and that level is high; (ii) either that situation is classified as "external_deployment" or that situation is classified as "internal_deployment", or both.
## f2-security-developed | always | 2.2: “Covered systems that cross this capability threshold are required to have robust and effective safeguards that sufficiently minimize the associated risk of severe harm before they are deployed and appropriate security controls as they are developed.”; its second object, security controls “as they are developed”
Rule 1. Every situation must have appropriate security controls during its development, if all of the following hold: (a) that situation is covered; (b) all of the following hold: (i) there is a level such that both that situation has reached that level and that level is high; (ii) that situation is classified as "further_development".
## f2-no-deploy | RS3=ext | 1: “We do not deploy models that reach a High capability threshold until the associated risks that they pose are sufficiently minimized.” (RS3=ext; RS0 narrowed by N1)
Rule 1. Every situation must not be deployed, if all of the following hold: (a) all of the following hold: (i) there is a level such that both that situation has reached that level and that level is high; (ii) that situation is classified as "external_deployment"; (b) it is not the case that that situation is classified as "sufficient_safeguards".
## f2-no-deploy-any | RS3=any | 1: “We do not deploy models that reach a High capability threshold until the associated risks that they pose are sufficiently minimized.” (RS3=any; RS0 narrowed by N1)
Rule 1. Every situation must not be deployed, if all of the following hold: (a) all of the following hold: (i) there is a level such that both that situation has reached that level and that level is high; (ii) either that situation is classified as "external_deployment" or that situation is classified as "internal_deployment", or both; (b) it is not the case that that situation is classified as "sufficient_safeguards".
## f3-develop | RS18=devonly | 4.4 (heading “Increasing safeguards before internal use and further development”): “Models that have reached or are forecasted to reach Critical capability in a Tracked Category present severe dangers and should be treated with extreme caution. Such models require additional safeguards (safety and security controls) during development, regardless of whether or when they are externally deployed.” (RS18=devonly: the body; no coverage condition, as the sentence states none; the act keeps the body's additional safety and security controls with the sufficiency of 4.2 and Appendix C, PLAN 12 item 22)
Rule 1. Every situation must have sufficient additional safety plus security controls during development, if all of the following hold: (a) there is a level such that both that situation has reached that level and that level is critical; (b) that situation is classified as "further_development".
## f3-develop-forecast | RS18=devonly | 4.4 (heading “Increasing safeguards before internal use and further development”): “Models that have reached or are forecasted to reach Critical capability in a Tracked Category present severe dangers and should be treated with extreme caution. Such models require additional safeguards (safety and security controls) during development, regardless of whether or when they are externally deployed.” (forecast branch; RS18=devonly: the body; no coverage condition, as the sentence states none; the act keeps the body's additional safety and security controls with the sufficiency of 4.2 and Appendix C, PLAN 12 item 22)
Rule 1. Every situation must have sufficient additional safety plus security controls during development, if all of the following hold: (a) that situation is classified as "forecast_critical"; (b) that situation is classified as "further_development".
## f3-develop-inuse | RS18=inuse | 4.4 (heading “Increasing safeguards before internal use and further development”): “Models that have reached or are forecasted to reach Critical capability in a Tracked Category present severe dangers and should be treated with extreme caution. Such models require additional safeguards (safety and security controls) during development, regardless of whether or when they are externally deployed.” (RS18=inuse: the body's development branch; the heading's internal use is f3-inuse)
Rule 1. Every situation must have sufficient additional safety plus security controls during development, if all of the following hold: (a) there is a level such that both that situation has reached that level and that level is critical; (b) that situation is classified as "further_development".
## f3-develop-inuse-forecast | RS18=inuse | 4.4 (heading “Increasing safeguards before internal use and further development”): “Models that have reached or are forecasted to reach Critical capability in a Tracked Category present severe dangers and should be treated with extreme caution. Such models require additional safeguards (safety and security controls) during development, regardless of whether or when they are externally deployed.” (forecast branch; RS18=inuse: the body's development branch; the heading's internal use is f3-inuse-forecast)
Rule 1. Every situation must have sufficient additional safety plus security controls during development, if all of the following hold: (a) that situation is classified as "forecast_critical"; (b) that situation is classified as "further_development".
## f3-inuse | RS18=inuse | 4.4 (heading “Increasing safeguards before internal use and further development”): “Models that have reached or are forecasted to reach Critical capability in a Tracked Category present severe dangers and should be treated with extreme caution. Such models require additional safeguards (safety and security controls) during development, regardless of whether or when they are externally deployed.” (RS18=inuse: the heading's internal use, its own act; reached branch)
Rule 1. Every situation must have additional safety as well as security controls before internal use, if all of the following hold: (a) there is a level such that both that situation has reached that level and that level is critical; (b) that situation is classified as "internal_deployment".
## f3-inuse-forecast | RS18=inuse | 4.4 (heading “Increasing safeguards before internal use and further development”): “Models that have reached or are forecasted to reach Critical capability in a Tracked Category present severe dangers and should be treated with extreme caution. Such models require additional safeguards (safety and security controls) during development, regardless of whether or when they are externally deployed.” (RS18=inuse: the heading's internal use, its own act; forecast branch)
Rule 1. Every situation must have additional safety as well as security controls before internal use, if all of the following hold: (a) that situation is classified as "forecast_critical"; (b) that situation is classified as "internal_deployment".
## f3-reminder | always | Appendix C: “Systems that reach Critical capability also require sufficient safeguards during development.” (no coverage condition: unlike the High sentence before it, this sentence states none; it restates 4.4 and 4.2 and reads their shared act, whose safeguards are sufficient and, as this sentence's also says, additional to those required before deployment)
Rule 1. Every situation must have sufficient additional safety plus security controls during development, if all of the following hold: (a) there is a level such that both that situation has reached that level and that level is critical; (b) that situation is classified as "further_development".
## f4-security | always | C.3: “We will require the following practices for High capability models”; no coverage condition
Rule 1. Every situation must have security practices, if there is a level such that both that situation has reached that level and that level is high.
## f7-evaluations | always | 3.3: “Prior to deployment, every covered model undergoes the suite of Scalable Evaluations.”; temporal relation remains ET-blocked
Rule 1. Every situation must undergo evaluations before deployment, if that situation is covered.
## f7-report | always | 3.3: “The SAG reviews the Capabilities Report and decides on next steps.”
Rule 1. Every situation must have SAG review the Capabilities Report as well as decide on next steps, if that situation is covered.
## f7-compile | always | 3.3: “The results of these evaluations, and any noteworthy observations that should affect interpretation of the results, are compiled into a Capabilities Report that is submitted to the SAG.” (after “Prior to deployment, every covered model undergoes the suite of Scalable Evaluations.”)
Rule 1. Every situation must have results compiled into a Capabilities Report submitted to the SAG, if that situation is covered.
## f7-recommend | RS16=coupled | 3.3: “The SAG reviews the Capabilities Report and decides on next steps. These can include: • Determine that the capability threshold has been crossed, and therefore recommend implementing and assessing corresponding safeguards, if they have not already been implemented and assessed.” (RS16=coupled)
Rule 1. Every situation must have the SAG recommend implementing as well as assessing the safeguards, if all of the following hold: (a) that situation is classified as "threshold_determined"; (b) it is not the case that that situation is classified as "safeguards_already_assessed".
## f8-start | always | 4: “If a covered system appears likely to cross a capability threshold, we will start to work on safeguards to sufficiently reduce the risks of severe harm associated with that threshold even if a formal capability determination has not yet been made.”
Rule 1. Every situation must start safeguard work to sufficiently reduce the threshold's severe-harm risk, if both that situation is covered and that situation is classified as "likely_to_cross".
## f9-report | always | 4: “Safeguards that mitigate the risk of severe harm are at the core of our safe deployment approach for covered systems that reach a capability threshold.” 4.2: “We compile the information on the planned safeguards needed to minimize the risk of severe harm into a Safeguards Report.”
Rule 1. Every situation must have planned safeguard information compiled into a Safeguards Report, if all of the following hold: (a) that situation is covered; (b) there is a level such that that situation has reached that level.
## f9-residual-strict | RS9=strict | 4.2: “The Safeguards Report should include the following information: • Identified ways a risk of severe harm can be realized for the given deployment, each mapped to the associated security controls and safeguards • Details about the efficacy of those safeguards • An assessment on the residual risk of severe harm based on the deployment • Any notable limitations with the information provided” (the third bullet)
Rule 1. Every situation must include an assessment of residual severe-harm risk in the Safeguards Report, if that situation is classified as "safeguards_report_compiled".
## f9-residual-default | RS9=default | 4.2: “The Safeguards Report should include the following information: • Identified ways a risk of severe harm can be realized for the given deployment, each mapped to the associated security controls and safeguards • Details about the efficacy of those safeguards • An assessment on the residual risk of severe harm based on the deployment • Any notable limitations with the information provided” (the third bullet; RS9=default)
Rule 1. Every situation should include an assessment of residual severe-harm risk in the Safeguards Report, if that situation is classified as "safeguards_report_compiled".
## f9-risk-paths | always | 4.1: “We use the following process to select safeguards for a deployment: • We first identify the plausible ways in which the associated risk of severe harm can come to fruition in the proposed deployment.”
Rule 1. Every situation must identify plausible ways severe harm can arise in the proposed deployment, if that situation is classified as "safeguard_selection".
## f9-specific | always | 4.1: “• For each of those, we then identify specific safeguards that either exist or should be implemented that would address the risk.”; per-item quantification remains SC-blocked
Rule 1. Every situation must identify specific safeguards that would address each risk path, if that situation is classified as "safeguard_selection".
## f9-efficacy | always | 4.1: “For each identified safeguard, we identify methods to measure their efficacy”; per-item quantification remains SC-blocked
Rule 1. Every situation must identify efficacy methods per safeguard, if that situation is classified as "safeguard_selection".
## f9-threshold | always | 4.1: “and an efficacy threshold” for each safeguard; per-item quantification remains SC-blocked
Rule 1. Every situation must identify an efficacy threshold per safeguard, if that situation is classified as "safeguard_selection".
## f10-validate | always | 4.2: “If we find reasonable evidence that our safeguards are not working as expected, we will validate the information being received and review the sufficiency of our safeguards.” (its first duty)
Rule 1. Every situation must validate the evidence, if that situation is classified as "reasonable_evidence".
## f10-review | always | 4.2: “If we find reasonable evidence that our safeguards are not working as expected, we will validate the information being received and review the sufficiency of our safeguards.” (its second duty)
Rule 1. Every situation must review safeguard sufficiency, if that situation is classified as "reasonable_evidence".
## f14-document | always | 5.1: “We will document relevant reports made to the SAG and of SAG’s decision and reasoning.”
Rule 1. Every situation must have reports to SAG as well as SAG decisions with reasoning documented.
## f14-investigate | always | 5.1: “We will track and appropriately investigate any reported or otherwise identified potential instances of noncompliance with this policy, and where reports are substantiated, will take appropriate and proportional corrective action.” (its first clause)
Rule 1. Every situation must have potential noncompliance tracked as well as appropriately investigated, if that situation is classified as "potential_noncompliance".
## f14-corrective | always | 5.1: “We will track and appropriately investigate any reported or otherwise identified potential instances of noncompliance with this policy, and where reports are substantiated, will take appropriate and proportional corrective action.” (its second clause)
Rule 1. Every situation must take appropriate as well as proportional corrective action, if that situation is classified as "substantiated_noncompliance".
## f14-summary | RS13=permission | 5.1: “Employees may also request and receive a summary of the testing results and SAG recommendation on capability levels and safeguards (subject to certain limits for highly sensitive information).” (the limits withdraw only what goes beyond them; the permission covers the request itself, so no request is its condition)
Rule 1. Every situation may have employees request as well as receive the testing as well as SAG summary, unless that situation is classified as "beyond_sensitive_limits".
## f15-disclose | always | 5.2: “We will release information about our Preparedness Framework results in order to facilitate public awareness of the state of frontier AI capabilities for major deployments. This published information will include the scope of testing performed, capability evaluations for each Tracked Category, our reasoning for the deployment decision, and any other context about a model’s development or capabilities that was decisive in the decision to deploy.” (the content list and the redaction sentence are in the act's legend)
Rule 1. Every situation must have Preparedness Framework results publicly released, if that situation is classified as "major_deployment".
## f15-safeguards | always | 5.2: “Additionally, if the model is beyond a High threshold, we will include information about safeguards we have implemented to sufficiently minimize the associated risks. Such disclosures about results and safeguards may be redacted or summarized where necessary, such as to protect intellectual property or safety.” (for major deployments; the redaction sentence limits the content, not the duty: the act's legend)
Rule 1. Every situation must disclose safeguards implemented to sufficiently minimize associated risks, if all of the following hold: (a) that situation is classified as "major_deployment"; (b) there is a level such that both that situation has reached that level and that level is high.
## f15-third-party | always | 5.2: “If we deem that a deployment warrants deeper testing of Tracked Categories of capability (as described in Section 3.1), for example based on results of Capabilities Report presented to them, then when available and feasible, OpenAI will work with third-parties to independently evaluate models.”
Rule 1. Every situation must have third parties independently evaluate the model, if both that situation is classified as "deeper_testing_warranted" and that situation is classified as "third_party_available".
## f16-sag-recommend | always | Appendix B: “For each deployment under the Preparedness Framework, providing recommendations on potential next steps and any applicable risks to OpenAI Leadership, as well as rationale”
Rule 1. Every situation must have SAG next-step recommendations with risks as well as rationale sent, if both that situation is covered and that situation is classified as "framework_deployment".
## c2-misalignment-strict | RS9=strict | C.2: “Safeguards should sufficiently minimize the risk associated with a misaligned model circumventing human control and oversight and executing severe harms.” (RS9=strict reads should as must; no applicability condition: the sentence states none, so the duty does not wait for a finding that the risk exists, and the means C.2 lists after it stay factors, PLAN 12 item 9)
Rule 1. Every situation must have safeguards that sufficiently minimize misaligned-model severe-harm risk.
## c2-misalignment-default | RS9=default | C.2: “Safeguards should sufficiently minimize the risk associated with a misaligned model circumventing human control and oversight and executing severe harms.” (RS9=default; no applicability condition: the sentence states none, so the duty does not wait for a finding that the risk exists, and the means C.2 lists after it stay factors, PLAN 12 item 9)
Rule 1. Every situation should have safeguards that sufficiently minimize misaligned-model severe-harm risk.
## c3-access-strict | RS9=strict | C.3: “For all models, especially those presenting High capabilities, access must be strictly limited, protected with strong multi-factor authentication, and may require additional approvals for access.” (no coverage condition, as the sentence states none: it is for all models; the “may require” clause is not translated, because it states a possibility, that access may need additional approvals, not a duty, and it grants no one a permission, so no rule of either reading carries it; PLAN 3.3)
Rule 1. Every situation must have model access strictly limited with strong multifactor authentication.
## c3-access-default | RS9=default | C.3: “For all models, especially those presenting High capabilities, access must be strictly limited, protected with strong multi-factor authentication, and may require additional approvals for access.” (no coverage condition, as the sentence states none: it is for all models; the “may require” clause is not translated, because it states a possibility, that access may need additional approvals, not a duty, and it grants no one a permission, so no rule of either reading carries it; PLAN 3.3)
Rule 1. Every situation should have model access strictly limited with strong multifactor authentication.
## cl4-persuasion | RS15=rule | Change log item 4: “Going forward we will handle risks related to persuasion outside the Preparedness Framework, including via our Model Spec and policy prohibitions on the use of our tools for political campaigning or lobbying, and our ongoing investigations of misuse of our products (including detecting and disrupting influence operations).” (RS15=rule; the means it lists are in the act's legend)
Rule 1. Every situation must have persuasion risks handled outside the Preparedness Framework, if that situation is classified as "persuasion_risk".
## f16-ssc-visibility | always | Appendix B: “The Safety and Security Committee (SSC) of the OpenAI Board of Directors (Board) will be given visibility into processes, and can review decisions and otherwise require reports and information from OpenAI Leadership as necessary to fulfill the Board’s oversight role.” (its first clause)
Rule 1. Every situation must give SSC visibility into processes.
## f16-informed | RS6=req | Appendix B: “Making all final decisions, including accepting any residual risks and making deployment go/no-go decisions, informed by SAG’s recommendations.” (RS6=req)
Rule 1. Every situation must have Leadership informed by SAG, if that situation is classified as "leadership_decision".
## f16-without-noinput | RS7=noinput | Appendix B: “For the avoidance of doubt, OpenAI Leadership can also make decisions without the SAG’s participation, i.e., the SAG does not have the ability to “filibuster”” (RS7=noinput: without any SAG recommendation)
Rule 1. Every situation need not have Leadership informed by SAG, if both that situation is classified as "leadership_decision" and that situation is classified as "decided_without_sag".
## f16-without-nodelib | RS7=nodelib | Appendix B: “For the avoidance of doubt, OpenAI Leadership can also make decisions without the SAG’s participation, i.e., the SAG does not have the ability to “filibuster”” (RS7=nodelib: without the SAG taking part in the decision)
Rule 1. Every situation need not have the SAG take part in the decision, if both that situation is classified as "leadership_decision" and that situation is classified as "decided_without_sag".
## f16-board-reverse | RS17=bperm | Appendix B: “Where necessary, the Board may reverse a decision and/or mandate a revised course of action.” (RS17=bperm: its first alternative)
Rule 1. Every situation may have the decision reversed by the Board, if both that situation is classified as "leadership_decision" and that situation is classified as "board_finds_necessary".
## f16-board-mandate | RS17=bperm | Appendix B: “Where necessary, the Board may reverse a decision and/or mandate a revised course of action.” (RS17=bperm: its second alternative)
Rule 1. Every situation may have a revised course of action mandated by the Board, if both that situation is classified as "leadership_decision" and that situation is classified as "board_finds_necessary".
## f16-chair-strict | RS9=strict | Appendix B: “In the rare case that a risk of severe harm rapidly develops (e.g., there is a change in our understanding of model safety that requires urgent response), we can request a fast track for the SAG to process the report urgently. The SAG Chair should also coordinate with OpenAI Leadership for immediate reaction as needed to address the risk.” (requesting the fast track is optional; “also” rests on the rapid-risk case)
Rule 1. Every situation must have the SAG Chair coordinate with OpenAI Leadership on immediate reaction, if both that situation is classified as "rapid_risk" and that situation is classified as "immediate_reaction_needed".
## f16-chair-default | RS9=default | Appendix B: “In the rare case that a risk of severe harm rapidly develops (e.g., there is a change in our understanding of model safety that requires urgent response), we can request a fast track for the SAG to process the report urgently. The SAG Chair should also coordinate with OpenAI Leadership for immediate reaction as needed to address the risk.” (RS9=default; requesting the fast track is optional)
Rule 1. Every situation should have the SAG Chair coordinate with OpenAI Leadership on immediate reaction, if both that situation is classified as "rapid_risk" and that situation is classified as "immediate_reaction_needed".
## f16-year | RS0=commitment | Appendix B: “We will review and potentially update the Preparedness Framework for continued sufficiency at least once a year.” (the review only, once, of the Framework: its sort is the Framework; “potentially update” is no duty)
Rule 1. Every framework must be reviewed at least once a year as to its continued sufficiency.
## f5-ambiguous | always | 3.2: “In cases of ambiguity about whether a model is covered by the Preparedness Framework, the SAG is responsible for making the final determination.”
Rule 1. Every situation must have SAG make the final coverage determination, if that situation is classified as "coverage_ambiguous".
## f5-checkpoint | always | 3.2: “If justified by our forecasts and threat models as potentially posing a severe risk during development and prior to external deployment, we will select an appropriate checkpoint during development to be covered by the Preparedness Framework.”; timing remains open
Rule 1. Every situation must have an appropriate development checkpoint selected to be covered, if that situation is classified as "forecast_severe_before_deployment".
## f6-rel | RS8=rel | footnote 6: “In general, models that we distill, fine-tune, or quantize from a model that was previously determined not to cross a High capability threshold will ordinarily not require additional safety measures barring reason to believe a significant increase in capability has occurred.”
Rule 1. Every situation generally need not require additional safety measures, if that situation is classified as "distilled_finetuned_or_quantized_from_below_high", unless that situation is classified as "significant_increase".
## f11-within | RS5=within | 4.3: “If we are able to rigorously confirm that such a scenario has occurred, then we could adjust accordingly the level of safeguards that we require in that capability area, but only if: • we assess that doing so does not meaningfully increase the overall risk of severe harm, • we publicly acknowledge that we are making the adjustment, • and, in order to avoid a race to the bottom on safety, we keep our safeguards at a level more protective than the other AI developer, and share information to validate this claim.” (RS5=within: an adjusted level must still sufficiently minimize the risk)
Rule 1. Every situation may have its required safeguards in that capability area adjusted, if both that situation is classified as "adjustment_confirmed" and that situation is classified as "adjustment_conditions".
## f11-exception | RS5=exception | 4.3: “If we are able to rigorously confirm that such a scenario has occurred, then we could adjust accordingly the level of safeguards that we require in that capability area, but only if: • we assess that doing so does not meaningfully increase the overall risk of severe harm, • we publicly acknowledge that we are making the adjustment, • and, in order to avoid a race to the bottom on safety, we keep our safeguards at a level more protective than the other AI developer, and share information to validate this claim.” (RS5=exception: an exception to F2's safeguard requirement in that capability area only: it holds when a level the situation has reached is in an area whose scenario was confirmed, unless another level it has reached is in no such area, so a level reached in another area keeps F2's duty)
Rule 1. Every situation need not have safeguards sufficiently minimizing severe-harm risk before deployment, if all of the following hold: (a) both that situation is classified as "adjustment_confirmed" and that situation is classified as "adjustment_conditions"; (b) there is a level such that all of the following hold: (i) that situation has reached that level; (ii) there is a category such that both that level concerns that category and that situation adjusts that category, unless there is a level such that all of the following hold: (a) that situation has reached that level; (b) it is not the case that there is a category such that both that level concerns that category and that situation adjusts that category.
## f11-exception-deploy | RS5=exception | 4.3: “If we are able to rigorously confirm that such a scenario has occurred, then we could adjust accordingly the level of safeguards that we require in that capability area, but only if: • we assess that doing so does not meaningfully increase the overall risk of severe harm, • we publicly acknowledge that we are making the adjustment, • and, in order to avoid a race to the bottom on safety, we keep our safeguards at a level more protective than the other AI developer, and share information to validate this claim.” (RS5=exception: the exception also covers section 1’s “We do not deploy models that reach a High capability threshold until the associated risks that they pose are sufficiently minimized.”; VE orders rules only on one act, so the exception has a rule on each of F2's two acts; in that capability area only: the permission holds when a level the situation has reached is in an area whose scenario was confirmed, unless another level it has reached is in no such area, so a level reached in another area keeps section 1's prohibition)
Rule 1. Every situation may be deployed, if all of the following hold: (a) both that situation is classified as "adjustment_confirmed" and that situation is classified as "adjustment_conditions"; (b) there is a level such that all of the following hold: (i) that situation has reached that level; (ii) there is a category such that both that level concerns that category and that situation adjusts that category, unless there is a level such that all of the following hold: (a) that situation has reached that level; (b) it is not the case that there is a category such that both that level concerns that category and that situation adjusts that category.
## f11-acknowledge | always | 4.3: “If we are able to rigorously confirm that such a scenario has occurred, then we could adjust accordingly the level of safeguards that we require in that capability area, but only if: • we assess that doing so does not meaningfully increase the overall risk of severe harm, • we publicly acknowledge that we are making the adjustment, • and, in order to avoid a race to the bottom on safety, we keep our safeguards at a level more protective than the other AI developer, and share information to validate this claim.” (the second condition, on the marginal-risk adjustment)
Rule 1. Every situation must have its adjustment publicly acknowledged, if both that situation is classified as "adjustment_made" and that situation is classified as "adjustment_confirmed".
## f11-protect | always | 4.3: “If we are able to rigorously confirm that such a scenario has occurred, then we could adjust accordingly the level of safeguards that we require in that capability area, but only if: • we assess that doing so does not meaningfully increase the overall risk of severe harm, • we publicly acknowledge that we are making the adjustment, • and, in order to avoid a race to the bottom on safety, we keep our safeguards at a level more protective than the other AI developer, and share information to validate this claim.” (the third condition, on the marginal-risk adjustment)
Rule 1. Every situation must keep safeguards more protective than the other developer's, if both that situation is classified as "adjustment_made" and that situation is classified as "adjustment_confirmed".
## f11-share | always | 4.3: “If we are able to rigorously confirm that such a scenario has occurred, then we could adjust accordingly the level of safeguards that we require in that capability area, but only if: • we assess that doing so does not meaningfully increase the overall risk of severe harm, • we publicly acknowledge that we are making the adjustment, • and, in order to avoid a race to the bottom on safety, we keep our safeguards at a level more protective than the other AI developer, and share information to validate this claim.” (the third condition's information sharing, on the marginal-risk adjustment)
Rule 1. Every situation must have validation information shared, if both that situation is classified as "adjustment_made" and that situation is classified as "adjustment_confirmed".
## f12-lra | RS4=N & RS4s=scoped | Table 2, Long-range Autonomy: “If a model has High or Critical capabilities in any of the Tracked Categories, require a “misalignment” safeguards report (see Section 4.2).” (RS4s=scoped: the row's research capability)
Rule 1. Every situation must have a misalignment safeguards report, if all of the following hold: (a) that situation is classified as "lra_found"; (b) there is a level such that all of the following hold: (i) that situation has reached that level; (ii) either that level is high or that level is critical, or both.
## f12-lra-unscoped | RS4=N & RS4s=unscoped | Table 2, Long-range Autonomy: “If a model has High or Critical capabilities in any of the Tracked Categories, require a “misalignment” safeguards report (see Section 4.2).” (RS4s=unscoped: only the stated condition)
Rule 1. Every situation must have a misalignment safeguards report, if there is a level such that all of the following hold: (a) that situation has reached that level; (b) either that level is high or that level is critical, or both.
## f12-sandbagging | RS4=N & RS4s=scoped | Table 2, Sandbagging: “Adopt elicitation approach that overcomes sandbagging, or use a conservative upper bound of the model’s non-sandbagged evaluation results” (one duty whose act's legend states both alternatives; RS4s=scoped: the row's research capability)
Rule 1. Every situation must have sandbagging handled, if that situation is classified as "sandbagging_found".
## f12-sandbagging-unscoped | RS4=N & RS4s=unscoped | Table 2, Sandbagging: “Adopt elicitation approach that overcomes sandbagging, or use a conservative upper bound of the model’s non-sandbagged evaluation results” (one duty whose act's legend states both alternatives; RS4s=unscoped: no stated condition)
Rule 1. Every situation must have sandbagging handled.
## f12-ara | RS4=N & RS4s=scoped | Table 2, Autonomous Replication and Adaptation: “Convert Autonomous Replication and Adaptation to a Tracked Category” (RS4s=scoped: the row's research capability)
Rule 1. Every situation must have the research category tracked, if that situation is classified as "ara_found".
## f12-ara-unscoped | RS4=N & RS4s=unscoped | Table 2, Autonomous Replication and Adaptation: “Convert Autonomous Replication and Adaptation to a Tracked Category” (RS4s=unscoped: no stated condition)
Rule 1. Every situation must have the research category tracked.
## f12-undermining | RS4=N & RS4s=scoped | Table 2, Undermining Safeguards: “If a model has High or Critical capabilities in any of the Tracked Categories, require the Safeguards case to be robust to the discovered capability and/or propensity” (RS4s=scoped: the row's research capability)
Rule 1. Every situation must have a safeguards case robust to what was discovered, if all of the following hold: (a) that situation is classified as "undermining_found"; (b) there is a level such that all of the following hold: (i) that situation has reached that level; (ii) either that level is high or that level is critical, or both.
## f12-undermining-unscoped | RS4=N & RS4s=unscoped | Table 2, Undermining Safeguards: “If a model has High or Critical capabilities in any of the Tracked Categories, require the Safeguards case to be robust to the discovered capability and/or propensity” (RS4s=unscoped: only the stated condition)
Rule 1. Every situation must have a safeguards case robust to what was discovered, if there is a level such that all of the following hold: (a) that situation has reached that level; (b) either that level is high or that level is critical, or both.
## f12-nuclear | RS4=N & RS4s=scoped | Table 2, Nuclear and Radiological: “Heighten safeguards (and consider further actions) in consultation with appropriate US government actors, accounting for the complexity of classified information handling.” (RS4s=scoped: the row's research capability)
Rule 1. Every situation must heighten safeguards consulting appropriate US government actors, if that situation is classified as "nuclear_found".
## f12-nuclear-unscoped | RS4=N & RS4s=unscoped | Table 2, Nuclear and Radiological: “Heighten safeguards (and consider further actions) in consultation with appropriate US government actors, accounting for the complexity of classified information handling.” (RS4s=unscoped: no stated condition)
Rule 1. Every situation must heighten safeguards consulting appropriate US government actors.
## f17-suff | RS12=suff | 2.1: “We treat a frontier capability as a Tracked Category if the capability creates a risk that meets five criteria:” (RS12=suff: the five criteria are sufficient)
Rule 1. Every capability must be tracked as a category, if all of the following hold: (a) that capability is plausible; (b) all of the following hold: (i) that capability is measurable; (ii) all of the following hold: (a) that capability is severe; (b) both that capability is classified as "net_new" and that capability is irremediable.
## f17-iff | RS12=iff | 2.1: “We treat a frontier capability as a Tracked Category if the capability creates a risk that meets five criteria:” (RS12=iff, with 2.3: the five criteria read as necessary and sufficient for Tracked Category treatment)
Rule 1. Every capability must be tracked as a category, if all of the following hold: (a) that capability is plausible; (b) all of the following hold: (i) that capability is measurable; (ii) all of the following hold: (a) that capability is severe; (b) both that capability is classified as "net_new" and that capability is irremediable.
## f17-iff-only | RS12=iff | 2.1: “We treat a frontier capability as a Tracked Category if the capability creates a risk that meets five criteria:” (RS12=iff, with 2.3: a missing criterion precludes Tracked Category treatment)
Rule 1. Every capability must not be tracked as a category, if it is not the case that all of the following hold: (a) that capability is plausible; (b) all of the following hold: (i) that capability is measurable; (ii) all of the following hold: (a) that capability is severe; (b) both that capability is classified as "net_new" and that capability is irremediable.
## f13-review | always | 2.3: “We will periodically review the latest research and findings for each Research Category.”; periodicity remains ET-blocked
Rule 1. Every situation must review the latest research as well as findings in each Research Category.
## f13-results | always | 2.3: “SAG will receive the results of such reviews and evaluate whether there is sufficient evidence to recommend updates to our internal practices or to the Preparedness Framework.”; assessment remains AP-blocked
Rule 1. Every situation must have SAG receive the research results, if that situation is classified as "research_category_review".
## cl-levels | always | Change log item 2: “Also, we are removing terms “low” and “medium” from the Framework, because those levels were not operationally involved in the execution of our Preparedness work.” (the “because” clause is a reason, not translated)
No level is low. No level is medium.
## fn5-indicator | always | footnote 5: “Given the higher potential severity of biological threats relative to chemical ones, we will prioritize Biological capability evaluations and these will be used as indicators for High and Critical capabilities for the category.” (the indicator relation only; the “we will prioritize” duty stays UNFORMALISED)
"biological_capability_evaluations" indicates "biological_and_chemical_high". "biological_capability_evaluations" indicates "biological_and_chemical_critical".
